  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nShallow moisture profilers are observed in forecast soundings \nwhich indicate less chances for accumulating snow across the \narea and in the mountains, especially with warm ground\ntemperatures. Even though some snow will sneak into the higher \nelevations, not expecting much for accumulations and generally \ntotals will stay around 1 inch with the potential for localized\nhigher amounts along the peaks and ridges of up to 2 inches. \nThis is designated for elevations above 2,500 feet. \nPrecipitation chances will continue through the morning then \ntaper off by early to mid afternoon. The area should then stay \ndry until tonight when additional chances come back into play \nfor the northeast mountains with a moisture flux off of Lake \nErie.\n\nThe parent upper level trough of this system moves off to the \neast while providing modest northwesterly flow which will aid \nin additional chances for accumulating snow albeit not \nexpecting much. These chances for snow showers will mainly be \nconfined in the northeast mountains and remain on and off late \ntonight through Sunday evening with little in the way of an \nabundant moisture flux to provide any significant accumulations.\nThis lack of a good moisture flux will limit additional snow \namounts to little to none. \n\nThis northwesterly flow advects in cooler air that will drop \ntemperatures into well below seasonable for this time of year \nwith temperatures being the coldest of the season thus far. \nThese colder temperatures will continue through midweek until \nfinally moderating back to normal. High pressure will dominate \nthe rest of Sunday and continue through the entire new work \nweek.",
